cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Did hydration breaks change the 2026 World Cup? A reproducible analysis of 203 mandatory breaks"
message: "If you use this analysis, its data tables or its figures, please cite it."
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: Baghestani
    given-names: Reza
    alias: rezaba002
repository-code: "https://github.com/rezaba002/WC2026-HydrationBreakAnalysis"
abstract: >-
  An independent, preregistered analysis of the 203 mandatory hydration breaks
  at the 2026 FIFA World Cup. Using a randomized pseudo-break design on a
  break-adjusted clock, the study finds no unusually large average change in
  shot balance after breaks: the apparent post-break shot drought is largely an
  artifact of counting stopped time as football. Structural changes were
  measurable instead — substitutions displaced to the restart, a fresh-legs
  deployment window, and longer matches. A preregistered perception dataset,
  collected via a stratified random sweep and verified against source, finds
  public claims attached to only about 8% of breaks while being supported
  roughly half the time, indicating an availability-sampling effect rather than
  mistaken observers.
keywords:
  - football
  - soccer
  - FIFA World Cup 2026
  - hydration breaks
  - matched counterfactual analysis
  - placebo design
  - sports analytics
  - reproducible research
